From 6bf1ad3c868f376784da8e67259d387f376b43c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vairamani muthusamy <vairamani.muthusamy@emerson.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 04:26:17 +0530
Subject: opkg: add post_invoke transaction hook

Add a "post_invoke" configuration option which names a hook to run after
a successful, state-changing opkg transaction (install, upgrade, remove,
configure or flag).

The value may be a single executable, or a directory of executables that
are run in lexical, run-parts-like order (a "drop-in" directory). This
lets integrators react to package changes -- for example to rebuild an
intrusion-detection baseline or refresh a cache -- without having to
wrap every opkg invocation.

The hook fires once per invocation, from the command-line frontend only,
and is gated on opkg_state_changed so it is not run for "opkg update",
for no-op transactions, for --noaction (dry-run) runs, or when the
transaction fails. libopkg API consumers are unaffected. Hooks are also
skipped when opkg operates on an offline root other than "/" (-o,
offline_root or OFFLINE_ROOT): they act on the running system, and
running them for another root would execute that root's files on the
host.

Dotfiles and non-executable directory entries are skipped, mirroring
standard drop-in directory semantics.

Because hooks run with opkg's (root) privileges, a hook -- and the
drop-in directory containing it -- is run only if it is owned by the
user opkg runs as and is not writable by group or other; otherwise it is
skipped with a log message. This prevents a less-privileged user from
substituting the code opkg executes.

To keep that check from being bypassed by swapping a pathname between
validation and execution, the configured path is opened one component at
a time with openat(O_NOFOLLOW) from "/": symlinks are refused, and every
directory on the way must be owned by root or the invoking user and must
not let another user rename its entries (not group/world-writable, or
sticky with the entry owned by root or that user). Each hook is opened
relative to the held directory descriptor, verified with fstat(), and
the same descriptor is executed with fexecve().

Each hook runs under a time limit (post_invoke_timeout, default 300 s)
in its own process group, measured against CLOCK_MONOTONIC in 64-bit
milliseconds so any configured value is honoured. On timeout opkg sends
SIGTERM; after a five-second grace it sends SIGKILL to any remaining
processes in that group, including children if the hook leader exits
first. A timeout is logged distinctly.

Hooks are best-effort: opkg logs each invocation and any non-zero exit
or timeout at a visible level, but a failing hook does not change opkg's
exit status and is not retried, since the transaction itself has already
completed.

Document the new options in opkg.conf.5 and add core tests covering
ordering, executable/trust filtering, symlink and writable-ancestor
rejection, dry-run/no-op/offline-root gating, best-effort failure
handling, and timeout cleanup, including a timeout too large for 32-bit
milliseconds. Since hooks do not run for offline roots, the post_invoke
tests drive opkg without -o and confine all of its paths to the test
root.

Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://lists.yoctoproject.org/g/opkg/message/221]
Differs from the submitted v3: also skips hooks for offline roots (-o)
and uses a 64-bit monotonic timeout deadline, from downstream review;
both will be in the next upstream revision.

